1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,560
In early 2018, Cape Town was weeks away
from turning off its taps.

2
00:00:05,580 --> 00:00:08,020
The city called it Day Zero.

3
00:00:08,040 --> 00:00:12,530
Four million people would have had to line up
for water at collection points.

4
00:00:12,620 --> 00:00:15,800
Meanwhile, about four thousand
kilometers to the south,

5
00:00:15,820 --> 00:00:18,640
Antarctica keeps breaking off icebergs.

6
00:00:18,660 --> 00:00:20,020
Giant ones.

7
00:00:20,040 --> 00:00:22,460
And an iceberg is fresh water.

8
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:26,740
It's made from snow that fell on land and was
pressed into ice over thousands

9
00:00:26,760 --> 00:00:27,950
of years.

10
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:29,900
So here's the obvious fix.

11
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:32,980
Send a few ships south, tie a
rope around an iceberg,

12
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:34,400
and tow it to the city.

13
00:00:34,420 --> 00:00:36,180
It sounds a little crazy.

14
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:40,473
But people have taken this idea
seriously for about fifty years.

15
00:00:40,493 --> 00:00:44,340
And the reason it hasn't happened has
nothing to do with ships.

16
00:00:44,360 --> 00:00:46,707
Let's start in 1977.

17
00:00:46,727 --> 00:00:51,927
A Saudi prince, Mohammed al-Faisal, wanted
fresh water for the Arabian Peninsula.

18
00:00:51,947 --> 00:00:55,500
He helped pay for an international
conference on icebergs at Iowa State

19
00:00:55,520 --> 00:00:56,473
University.

20
00:00:56,493 --> 00:01:00,740
To get attention, a chunk of glacier ice
was shipped from Alaska all the

21
00:01:00,760 --> 00:01:01,880
way to Iowa.

22
00:01:01,900 --> 00:01:05,960
The prince's company planned to tow an
Antarctic iceberg of about a hundred

23
00:01:05,980 --> 00:01:07,940
million tonnes to Arabia.

24
00:01:07,960 --> 00:01:12,940
In 1977, he was quoted saying it could
happen within three years.

25
00:01:12,960 --> 00:01:14,220
It never happened.

26
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:17,900
And the first problem is the part of
the iceberg you can't see.

27
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:21,480
About ninety percent of an iceberg
sits below the waterline.

28
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:24,160
The bit you see on top is only the tip.

29
00:01:24,180 --> 00:01:28,640
Researchers at the Woods Hole Oceanographic
Institution ran computer simulations

30
00:01:28,660 --> 00:01:30,267
of towing icebergs.

31
00:01:30,287 --> 00:01:34,160
They found that an iceberg big enough to
survive the trip had to be at least

32
00:01:34,180 --> 00:01:37,840
three hundred meters long and about
two hundred meters thick.

33
00:01:37,860 --> 00:01:39,760
Two hundred meters.

34
00:01:39,780 --> 00:01:42,667
That's about as tall as a
fifty-story building,

35
00:01:42,687 --> 00:01:44,640
mostly hanging under the surface.

36
00:01:44,660 --> 00:01:47,160
So the iceberg can't come into a harbor.

37
00:01:47,180 --> 00:01:50,373
It would hit the sea floor long
before it reached the coast.

38
00:01:50,393 --> 00:01:54,360
It has to be parked far out at sea, and the
water has to travel the rest

39
00:01:54,380 --> 00:01:55,280
of the way.

40
00:01:55,300 --> 00:01:57,360
Then there's the tow itself.

41
00:01:57,380 --> 00:02:01,807
Ships already tow icebergs today, off the
coast of Newfoundland in Canada.

42
00:02:01,827 --> 00:02:02,820
Not to drink them.

43
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:05,780
To push them away from
offshore oil platforms.

44
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:08,500
And those crews know how tricky it is.

45
00:02:08,520 --> 00:02:12,820
Icebergs are smooth and slippery, so the
rope can slide right off.

46
00:02:12,840 --> 00:02:15,700
And because most of the weight
sits deep underwater,

47
00:02:15,720 --> 00:02:19,210
pulling on the top can make the
whole iceberg roll over.

48
00:02:19,260 --> 00:02:21,680
Now picture doing that for weeks.

49
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:26,400
In the Woods Hole simulations, the tow moved
at about half a meter per second.

50
00:02:26,420 --> 00:02:28,620
That's slower than a slow walk.

51
00:02:28,640 --> 00:02:32,300
At that speed, reaching Cape Town
took about two months.

52
00:02:32,320 --> 00:02:35,750
And for the whole trip, the
iceberg is melting.

53
00:02:35,860 --> 00:02:39,140
Near Antarctica, the sea is
close to freezing.

54
00:02:39,220 --> 00:02:42,570
But the closer you get to Cape
Town, the warmer it gets.

55
00:02:42,840 --> 00:02:46,830
The sea off Cape Town is around fifteen
to twenty degrees Celsius.

56
00:02:47,080 --> 00:02:50,350
That's roughly sixty to
sixty-eight degrees Fahrenheit.

57
00:02:50,580 --> 00:02:53,370
Warm water eats away at the
ice from below.

58
00:02:53,640 --> 00:02:55,750
Waves wear down the sides.

59
00:02:55,800 --> 00:02:58,190
Pieces crack off and float away.

60
00:02:58,360 --> 00:03:01,550
That's why smaller icebergs in the
study didn't make it at all.

61
00:03:01,780 --> 00:03:03,640
They melted before they arrived.

62
00:03:03,660 --> 00:03:06,690
But the real trouble starts
when the trip ends.

63
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:09,460
Once the iceberg is parked off the coast,

64
00:03:09,480 --> 00:03:12,560
it sits in that warm water day and night.

65
00:03:12,580 --> 00:03:16,120
The study found that a parked
iceberg could melt in weeks,

66
00:03:16,140 --> 00:03:17,700
or even days.

67
00:03:17,720 --> 00:03:20,000
So how big would it need to be?

68
00:03:20,060 --> 00:03:24,920
To give Cape Town a full year of water,
without anything to slow the melting,

69
00:03:24,940 --> 00:03:29,400
the iceberg would have to be about three
kilometers long when it arrives.

70
00:03:29,420 --> 00:03:31,340
Three kilometers.

71
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:34,220
And it still has to start much
bigger than that,

72
00:03:34,240 --> 00:03:36,320
because so much is lost on the way.

73
00:03:36,380 --> 00:03:40,680
People have suggested wrapping the iceberg
in a giant skirt of fabric to

74
00:03:40,700 --> 00:03:42,130
shield it from the warm water.

75
00:03:42,360 --> 00:03:43,310
That could help.

76
00:03:43,620 --> 00:03:46,980
But nobody has tested it on an
iceberg that size.

77
00:03:47,040 --> 00:03:49,830
And that leads to the hardest
problem of all.

78
00:03:50,060 --> 00:03:53,890
Even if the iceberg arrives, how do
you get the water out?

79
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:58,550
When ice melts in the sea, the fresh water
doesn't stay in a neat puddle.

80
00:03:58,760 --> 00:04:01,230
It mixes into the salt water around it.

81
00:04:01,380 --> 00:04:04,320
And once it's mixed, you're
back to salty water.

82
00:04:04,380 --> 00:04:09,490
You could try to carve off blocks, or pump
the meltwater up before it mixes.

83
00:04:09,760 --> 00:04:13,030
But nobody has a proven way to do
that at this scale.

84
00:04:13,260 --> 00:04:18,360
In 2018, during the drought, a salvage
expert named Nick Sloane proposed

85
00:04:18,380 --> 00:04:20,560
exactly this plan for Cape Town.

86
00:04:20,580 --> 00:04:25,200
He estimated one big iceberg could supply
about a fifth to a third of the

87
00:04:25,220 --> 00:04:27,080
city's water for a year.

88
00:04:27,100 --> 00:04:31,640
By his own estimate, towing it would cost
about a hundred million dollars,

89
00:04:31,660 --> 00:04:34,480
plus tens of millions more to
collect the water.

90
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:36,090
The city said no.

91
00:04:36,300 --> 00:04:39,800
Officials said groundwater and
desalination were cheaper,

92
00:04:39,820 --> 00:04:43,330
or at least no more expensive,
and far less risky.

93
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:46,070
So what did Cape Town actually do?

94
00:04:46,260 --> 00:04:48,010
People saved water.

95
00:04:48,240 --> 00:04:49,270
Short showers.

96
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:50,790
No watering gardens.

97
00:04:50,960 --> 00:04:54,150
A limit of fifty liters per
person per day.

98
00:04:54,260 --> 00:04:57,260
The city cut its water use
roughly in half.

99
00:04:57,380 --> 00:04:59,320
Then the rain came back.

100
00:04:59,400 --> 00:05:01,720
Day Zero never arrived.

101
00:05:01,920 --> 00:05:04,230
So why don't we just tow an iceberg?

102
00:05:04,320 --> 00:05:08,550
Because most of it hides underwater, so
it can't come near the shore.

103
00:05:08,660 --> 00:05:12,250
Because it's slippery,
top-heavy, and slow to drag.

104
00:05:12,340 --> 00:05:16,480
Because it melts the whole way there, and
even faster once it's parked.

105
00:05:16,500 --> 00:05:20,560
And because nobody knows how to catch the
water before it disappears into

106
00:05:20,580 --> 00:05:21,410
the sea.

107
00:05:21,500 --> 00:05:22,890
The ice is free.

108
00:05:22,980 --> 00:05:25,370
Getting it into a tap is the hard part.

